@startuml
title Class Diagram - Real-Time Messaging Application

skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor<<planned>> White
  BorderColor<<planned>> Gray
  FontColor<<planned>> Gray
}

' ============================
' Implemented classes
' ============================

class User <<Django built-in>> {
  +id : Integer
  +username : String {unique}
  -password : String {hashed}
}

class Conversation {
  +id : Integer
  +created_at : DateTime
}

class Message {
  +id : Integer
  +content : String
  +created_at : DateTime
}

' ============================
' Planned classes (not yet in code)
' ============================

class Membership <<planned>> {
  +id : Integer
  +joined_at : DateTime
  +last_read_at : DateTime
}

' ============================
' Implemented associations
' ============================

User "0..* conversations" -- "2..* participants" Conversation : participates in >
Conversation "1 conversation" *-- "0..* messages" Message : contains >
User "1 author" -- "0..* messages" Message : writes >

' Association class attached to the User <-> Conversation membership
(User, Conversation) .. Membership

' ============================
' Notes
' ============================

note bottom of Membership
  <<planned>> association class, formalizing
  the User <-> Conversation many-to-many.
  Today this relation is a plain
  participants = ManyToManyField(User)
  on Conversation, with no join model.
  Django can migrate it to a through=
  model without breaking existing data,
  once unread counters or per-member
  join dates are needed.
end note

note right of Conversation
  Planned attributes (for groups):
  + type : String {enum: private, group}
  + name : String (optional, groups only)
end note

note bottom of Message
  Django mapping:
  author = ForeignKey(User, on_delete=CASCADE)
  conversation = ForeignKey(Conversation, on_delete=CASCADE)

  Planned attributes (if message editing
  is picked as the optional feature):
  + edited_at : DateTime
  + is_deleted : Boolean
end note

@enduml